WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  good afternoon and welcome to the arena  where on this day we honor the memory  of the drum major  i'm ernie johnson grateful to be here to  host an hour-long round table discussion  which will begin in just a few minutes  from the pulpit of ebenezer baptist  church here in atlanta shortly before  his death  dr martin luther king jr told the  congregation that he wanted to be  remembered as a servant and as a drum  major for justice for peace  for righteousness  we will reflect on his legacy today  but we begin with a visit to memphis  for years on this day on our show we've  spoken of the national civil rights  museum and i always found myself saying  you know i need to get there and see it  for myself  well last week i did  my wife cheryl and i found ourselves  wading into a sea of history  taking photographs  immersed in the stories of heroes  upon heroes  there are  inside the walls of the national civil  rights museum in memphis  chapters of the civil rights struggle  painful to the core at the same time  figures in bronze  stand as witness to courage  and resolve  faces of determination molded and cast  hearkening back to a time when they were  moved to action  and now they stand immovable  serving as testament to the solidarity  and sacrifice it took to enact change  far too long in coming  inside these walls there are stories so  many stories  i was introduced to clara looper  the oklahoma high school teacher who  organized lunch counter sit-ins  who was arrested two dozen times  who took her father's lifelong  frustration at being denied the right to  sit at a restaurant of his choosing and  said  tonight is the night  history compels us to go  let history alone be our final judge  ernest greene  one of the little rock nine  who enrolled at all white central high  and was the first to graduate  despite a written plea from a white  worried senior did he not attend the  ceremony  you deserve your sheepskin as much as  anyone else and i would not stop you  from getting it however there are  as you well know many who would gladly  do so  it will be a time that i and hundreds of  others do not wish to have marred  there is joanne robinson an english  professor at alabama state college who  on the day rosa parks was arrested for  not surrendering her seat on a  montgomery alabama bus went to an office  at the college and secretly mimeographed  thousands of flyers calling for a  one-day bus boycott  and distributed them throughout the city  you read words from the younger brother  of medgar evers hands that pick cotton  can now pick the mayor  as charles evers became the first  african-american mayor of a southern  city  since the days of reconstruction  ultimately your journey in memphis so  powerful  takes you to room 306 at the lorraine  motel  and while you know the story of april  4th 1968  there is this feeling  wish i could articulate it  i can't  of seeing that room and that balcony and  that boarding house across the street  where it is believed the shot was fired  that took the life of dr martin luther  king jr  and there is this  just moments before the deadly shot rang  out dr king  standing on the balcony  had been in conversation with  saxophonist ben branch on street level  he asked if branch would play his  favorite hymn precious lord take my hand  in an upcoming rally  and quote play it real pretty  branch's response we are told was  you know i will doc  there was so much to take in so much to  process in this visit it's overwhelming  and then as you make your way toward the  exit you see the work of sculptor  michael pavlovsky the movement to  overcome it stands in the museum's  atrium and encapsulates what you've just  spent hours experiencing a tribute to  the millions who have marched  who have struggled  who have climbed  ever upward

